irs of chromosomes  chromosome 12 22 the so-called autosomes  and then the sex chromosomes the  chromosome x and y so this is the  karyotype of a male this is the  technique that's used to for example  test for trisomy 21 that's associated  with Down syndrome more recently in 2007  what we call our micro razor gene chips  were introduced that have over a million  spots of DNA on a glass coverslip the  size of a quarter and using this  technology which is a form of  nanotechnology  you can take DNA from an individual and  tested in a few hours to look for the  presence and absence of specific genetic  variants across the human genome this  approach really revolutionized the way  we do genetic studies it allows to look  at the whole genome and one experiment  relatively cost efficiently and the new  technology is going to really change  everything and it is changing everything  is what we call whole genome sequencing  using next-generation sequencing  technologies so using this approach we  can now capture all six billion chemical  basis of information in a single  experiment we don't have to test one  gene of the 23,000 30,000 at a time we  can capture the sequence in one  experiment and in my laboratory now we  can do this for a cost of about  twenty-five hundred dollars or so the  human genome project cost three billion  dollars to complete the first human  genome sequence which was of craig  Venter in 2007 costs about 70 million  dollars and now we're doing it for  twenty-five hundred dollars of genome we  expect it will be a thousand dollars of  genome and it comes into the cost range  of Cary typing microwaves it will become  a standard clinical diagnostic test and  it's quite likely that each of us will  have our genome sequence as a step in  personalized medicine so these are the  technologies we're dealing with and here  I have summarized research results  generated over the past 30 years that  reveal the proportion roughly twenty  percent of the genetic arch