n  identified so I think it's important to  show this slide to re-emphasize now that  some of the CNVs copy number variants in  genes that have been identified are  considered to be the cause of autism in  the individuals that carry them these  six DNA segments represent examples of  the most common DNA regions found to be  involved in autism in our canadian  population diagnostic tests for these  seen v's and genes are now used in  conjunction with standard clinical  assessment one of the complexities of  the genetic findings is the fact that  some of the same genes are or regions  affect our being a found associated with  other neurodevelopmental conditions such  as schizophrenia bipolar disorder ADHD  and intellectual disability this  observation is perhaps not entirely  surprising because individuals and  families with autism can exhibit these  other types of disorders and perhaps  we'll talk more about this in the  discussion I will discuss the four genes  or seen v's highlighted in red later  with the shank one  mentioned on my next slide so on this  slide is an example of what we call a  pedigree for generation family with  female shown it circles and males as  squares individuals colored in black  have autism and all of them carry a  familial or inherited deletion of one  copy of their shank one gene which is  typically present in two copies one from  mom one from dad and this is on an  autism chromosome 19 so it's not the sex  chromosomes not the X chromosome I'll  talk about that a bit later the index  case which is the first presenting clay  case in the clinic is shown with the  arrow and as soon as can be seen he  inherits the deletion of shank one from  his mother for those a geneticist in the  crowd you'll probably quickly recognize  that only the male's will carry the  shank one deletion have autism while the  females do not we don't know the reason  for this yet but it may be that females  have other factors such as gender  specific hormones that pro